// The schedule matching WoSX's GPU solver: kernel/fcpwb.bonsai's traversal
// of FCPW's binary tree in FCPW's bytes, in the kernel's order, run as WoSX's
// `runPersistentPointEstimator` runs its walks (include/wosx/gpu/
// entrypoints/common-shaders/point-estimation.cs.slang, WoSX's default
// when the backend is CUDA) -- 131072 resident threads in groups of 256,
// each claiming ONE WALK at a time from a global counter until the walks
// are gone, the work item `k` naming the point as `k % points` and the
// walk as `k / points` so that consecutive claims are consecutive points,
// each thread's walk adding to its point's statistics (WoSX merges under a
// per-point spinlock; here the walk's sums are atomic adds into the point's
// slots).
//
// Derived from the program's two loops by four directives. `reorder(i, w)`
// puts the walks outside the points: what the point's body did around its
// walks -- the sums it starts, the mean it takes at the end -- is
// distributed into a loop before the nest and one after it
// (SSA/ReorderLoops.h), and the point's three sums become one slot per
// point. `collapse(w, i, k)` makes the perfect nest one loop over every
// (walk, point) pair, the point fastest. `persistent(k, t, 131072)` hands
// its iterations to 131072 workers claiming from a counter (Aila and Laine's
// persistent threads, WoSX's kernel's shape and count), and the workers are
// split into groups of 256 bound to the GPU's blocks and threads. The two
// distributed loops over the points stay where a `parfor` left unbound runs,
// on the host, before and after the launch.
import apps/wosx/schedules/kernel/fcpwb;

schedule {
    solve_all.reorder(i, w);
    solve_all.collapse(w, i, k);
    solve_all.persistent(k, t, 131072);
    solve_all.split(t, t_blk, t_thr, 256, true);
    solve_all.bind(t_blk, GPUBlock);
    solve_all.bind(t_thr, GPUThread);
}
